# Runtime operational data — never committed (charter §12.1, §12.2):
# contains mail content, personal data, and client material.
data/control.db
data/backup/**
!data/backup/.gitkeep
data/submissions/**
!data/submissions/.gitkeep
data/quarantine/**
!data/quarantine/.gitkeep
data/exports/**
!data/exports/.gitkeep
outbox/pending-approval/**
!outbox/pending-approval/.gitkeep
outbox/sent/**
!outbox/sent/.gitkeep
logs/**
!logs/.gitkeep
!logs/README.md

# Run transcripts. They carry mailbox scan output — senders, subjects,
# counts — which is the §12.2 personal data the rest of this file keeps
# out of git.
reports/runs/**
!reports/runs/.gitkeep

# Phase 0 output — the same rule, and the hole this file used to have.
#
# On the live laptop CONTROL_ROOT *is* the repo checkout, so Stage A-J
# write straight into this tracked directory: file-inventory.csv lists
# every path on the company drive, COMMERCIAL-EXPOSURE.md carries
# contract dates and guarantee values for NDA clients, GAP-ANALYSIS.md
# names individuals and their filing behaviour. One `git add -A` would
# push all of it to GitHub — §1.8, "never leak internal content
# externally", and §12.1/§12.2 in one move.
#
# The rule below only ever covered a CONTROL_ROOT *nested* inside the
# repo. The repo's own discovery/ was tracked.
discovery/**
!discovery/.gitkeep
!discovery/README.md

# A CONTROL_ROOT created inside the repo. Discovery output is mail
# metadata about identified individuals — senders, subjects, timestamps
# — and falls under the same rule as the paths above (§12.1, §12.2).
#
# Anchored to the repo root on purpose. Git on Windows and macOS matches
# ignore patterns case-insensitively, so an unanchored "CONTROL/" also
# swallows src/control/ — the source package — and new modules there
# would silently never be committed.
/CONTROL/

# Local environment
.env
*.pst
*.ost

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
